we go."], [228.5, "In the future, 10 years from now, in 10 years, there will be a class."], [235.5, "And in the class, there will be a student named Yuriko."], [239.1, "Yuriko will be unfit to be a student."], [242.2, "Every day she'll come to class late."], [245.1, "Then she'll belittle the other students."], [246.7, "When another student makes a mistake, Yuriko will yell, Are you stupid?"], [252.1, "Do you have a mental disability?"], [253.9, "What's wrong with you, you idiot?"], [256.8, "Now, one day, another student, named Jinhua, will complain to the teacher, whose name is AJ."], [262.8, "She'll say, I'm frightened by Yuriko."], [265.4, "I'm going to quit this class."], [267.2, "The teacher will say, Stick it out, Jinhua."], [270.5, "Don't worry."], [271.7, "I'll get rid of Yuriko."], [273.2, "Then we'll all be better off."], [275.3, "The next day, AJ will bring a gun to class."], [279.1, "He'll point the gun at Yuriko and threaten her."], [283.2, "Yuriko, if you come to my class again, I'll kill you and then I'll take custody of your son and beat him every day."], [290.1, "So leave now."], [291.8, "Yuriko will be frightened by AJ's threat."], [294.3, "She'll start crying and then she'll run out of the class."], [297.5, "The other students will cheer."], [299.1, "Yay!"], [300.3, "Yuriko's gone, yay!"], [302.7, "Alright, that's all for the point of view stories."], [306.8, "Please now go back."], [308.2, "Listen to the first one, then pause and try to tell the first story."], [313.6, "Yourself beginning with 20 years ago."], [316.5, "Try to tell all the story and use the correct verbs and the correct vocabulary."], [321.7, "Then listen to the second one, starting with since January."], [325.5, "Listen to it a few times and then pause and try to tell it yourself out loud, maybe be alone in your room or something."], [332.1, "And then finally."], [333.4, "Listen to the third one beginning with in 10 years or 10 years from now and try to tell that one yourself also."], [342.1, "If you have trouble, if you can't remember exactly the words to say or you make a few mistakes, listen, just relax."], [349.2, "Okay."], [349.8, "Relax."], [350.4, "It takes a little time."], [351.8, "The most important thing is to listen very carefully, and to listen again and again and again, until you are sleeping and dreaming about these stories."], [360.8, "Then it will become automatic."], [362.5, "So please relax about making mistakes; this is not a test, okay?"], [366.7, "In fact, you can only listen, if you want to."], [370.2, "If you want to just listen, you don't need to try to tell the stories yourself."], [375.7, "If it makes you feel stressed, if it makes you feel nervous or uncomfortable to pause and speak, then don't do it."], [383.3, "Only tell the stories if you are relaxed about it,"], [386.8, "if you can do it and you don't worry, you don't feel nervous about making mistakes."], [391.5, "If you feel nervous, just relax and listen only."], [394.2, "That's fine."], [395.4, "Okay, I'll see you next time."], [396.8, "Bye-bye."]]$j$::jsonb,
  $j$[[0.4, "Okay, this is the commentary for Lost Custody, the Dear Abby letter."], [5.8, "This is an interesting letter."], [8.1, "It reminds me of my old social work days."], [10.8, "Before I became an English teacher, I was a social worker."], [15.9, "And I had various jobs."], [18.0, "I worked with runaway teenagers, homeless teenagers for a while."], [24.1, "And I also worked for a short time in a government agency that would deal with custody, right?"], [32.0, "We would investigate children who had been abused and decide, you know, should the parents have custody or should the government take custody or should someone else have custody."], [43.9, "To be honest, I hated that job and I quit pretty quickly."], [48.3, "I did not last very long and that job is a horrible job."], [53.7, "But now, I did have some other very nice social work jobs."], [57.7, "In general, social work was fine, but that job in particular was pretty terrible."], [62.3, "As you can imagine, a lot of terrible situations for the children."], [66.2, "And sometimes I didn't agree; I didn't think that the government should take custody of some of the children."], [72.5, "Anyway, really kind of a terrible situation."], [74.8, "But anyway, in this letter,"], [78.2, "we've got this mother, young mother."], [80.6, "And she's worried about losing custody of her child, her son."], [85.3, "Now some of the people who write Dear Abby, you will see, they're a little strange."], [92.8, "Sometimes the letters, you know, they're kind of funny too actually sometimes."], [96.7, "It's a little bit like watching Jerry Springer."], [99.4, "Jerry Springer is a TV show here in America."], [102.2, "They get all these really crazy Americans with all these horrible, terrible problems, and they get on the show and they argue and yell at each other."], [110.5, "Well, Dear Abby is not that bad, but there's still a little bit of that in some of these letters, where you're kind of thinking, wow, these people are strange."], [119.9, "I thought this was strange, for example, when the woman says in the second paragraph, “On our honeymoon, Derek told me if I ever divorced him, that he'd make sure he would get custody of our son.”"], [131.9, "So that seems like a bad sign, doesn't it?"], [134.1, "They're on their honeymoon, they just got married, and already the husband's threatening her."], [139.5, "And saying he's going to take the son if they get a divorce."], [143.8, "Also from that sentence, I think we can realize that they already had a son when they got married."], [150.3, "So she must have gotten pregnant and had the baby before they got married."], [156.0, "Or else they wouldn't be talking about this on their honeymoon, right?"], [159.4, "So anyway, that's a bad sign, right?"], [161.5, "They're on the honeymoon and he's threatening her already, just on the honeymoon."], [166.9, "So, oh, bad sign."], [168.5, "And then his mother, the mother-in-law,"], [171.0, "also threatened her, saying, I'll tell the judge that you're an unfit mother if you ever try to leave my son."], [177.7, "So, uh-oh, these are warning signs to me, right?"], [180.5, "I think if I'm in a relationship and the mother-in-law's threatening me if I leave and the husband's threatening me if I leave."], [189.0, "Maybe that tells you something."], [190.2, "Maybe this guy's not such a good guy."], [192.4, "Maybe other women have left him many times in the past."], [198.6, "Maybe both the husband and the mom realize he's a bad guy and she probably will want to leave in the future."], [204.7, "I don't know, that's what I understood when I read that second paragraph."], [208.8, "I thought, uh-oh, bad sign."], [211.5, "Something's wrong already."], [213.8, "And then of course, we see that obviously there is something wrong: the husband belittles her all the time."], [220.4, "So, you know, when a husband belittles his wife,"], [223.5, "oh, that's pretty terrible, also a bad sign, right?"], [226.2, "He's saying terrible things insulting his wife."], [229.0, "Obviously, this is not a good guy."], [231.9, "Okay, so in that first paragraph, she says"], [234.7, "Derek refuses to support his family."], [237.3, "To support your family means to make money for your family."], [242.0, "You know, you buy food for them, you make money, you work."], [244.5, "We call all of that together, supporting your family."], [247.3, "So he's obviously, he doesn't have a job, not for a year, and he won't support his family."], [253.5, "Okay, so uh-oh, strike one, two, three."], [256.8, "Now, that's a little bit of slang, you probably know."], [260.7, "When we say strike one, strike two, strike three, that comes from baseball."], [265.7, "It's slang in American English, and if you get three strikes, it means you're finished, you're out, you're gone."], [273.0, "So these are three very bad signs, right?"], [276.1, "The mother-in-law is threatening, the husband's threatening, and he won't work."], [280.4, "Okay, anyway, then she says, my son is my world, meaning my son is everything for me."], [287.0, "He's everything in my life."], [288.9, "I don't care about anything else."], [290.4, "I only care about him."], [293.0, "And the son won't let his father hold him."], [296.8, "Okay, hold him in his arms."], [298.2, "That's also a bad sign."], [300.3, "But then the woman says, because I'm on disability,"], [304.1, "I don't know if I have a chance to get custody."], [308.8, "And then she says, what should I do?"], [310.2, "Should I stick it out with my husband, or should I take the chance of losing my son?"], [313.4, "Okay, now, as a social worker, I've worked in this kind of situation in this field before."], [320.4, "So this situation, we never know, right?"], [322.4, "With Dear Abby, we don't know the whole truth."], [325.0, "Only one person's writing, and they don't include all the information we need to give good advice."], [331.5, "Now, Abby doesn't care."], [332.5, "She gives advice anyway, but, you know, most of us would want to know more."], [336.7, "For example, she's on disability."], [338.5, "What does that mean?"], [340.1, "Well, because I've worked in social work, I know that disability might be something very serious, but I also know it could be something that's not serious."], [349.8, "In the United States, some people can get disability if they have a mental problem."], [355.6, "For example, they're very depressed."], [357.8, "They're medically depressed."], [359.7, "They're always depressed."], [361.1, "Well, sometimes they get a lawyer and they can get disability."], [364.5, "They can get money from the government because of that."], [367.2, "Sometimes people who are alcoholics or drug addicts can get disability."], [372.2, "They can get money from the government because they are alcoholics or drug addicts and they have a hard time working."], [379.5, "So obviously these are not such serious physical problems."], [383.7, "So this woman, we don't know, we have no idea."], [385.9, "She might be in a wheelchair and can't walk or she might be a former alcoholic."], [393.0, "We really don't know what the disability is."], [396.1, "And of course it would be a big, important point to know."], [400.6, "If she has disability because she has a mental problem that's very serious, maybe she's not a fit mother, maybe she would be an unfit mother."], [410.6, "However, if she has just some physical problem, obviously it's"], [414.4, "no problem at all, she could easily get her son."], [418.0, "So it's hard to know what to tell this woman, right?"], [422.0, "I would say in normal situations in the United States, the mother has an advantage with custody."], [428.6, "When they go to court, if everything is generally equal,"], [432.2, "the mother has a better chance to keep custody of the child."], [437.7, "Now is this fair?"], [438.6, "I don't know."], [439.1, "We talked about this in my class in San Francisco."], [441.6, "Some people said, well that's not fair!"], [443.5, "And I don't know if it's fair, but it's true."], [447.4, "I guess this has historical reasons."], [449.8, "People just have this idea that mothers take care of children, they're better at taking care of children than fathers."], [457.6, "Of course, that's not always true, but that is an idea that's in the United States, and that is in the legal system, even in the courts."], [468.2, "So a mother often has an advantage against a father if they get a divorce and both want custody."], [475.7, "The mother typically has an advantage."], [478.0, "Now, many other things are important also."], [481.2, "Their income, you know, what kind of person they are, that kind of thing."], [486.2, "So it's complicated."], [489.0, "But anyway, this guy sounds quite bad; he hasn't worked for a year."], [493.0, "He obviously has some problems."], [495.8, "Probably this mother would get custody."], [499.0, "Unless she has a serious, you know, mental problem."], [503.4, "My guess is that if she went to court, she would get custody of the child."], [508.4, "But anyway, but it's hard to know."], [510.5, "We don't know the details."], [513.1, "Well, okay, I guess I'll talk now a little bit about my social work background in general."], [520.9, "When I worked in this field of custody, I worked for a place called the Department of Family and Children Services in Georgia."], [529.9, "Georgia is a state in the United States."], [533.1, "Georgia's in the southeast, next to Florida; Atlanta is the big city in Georgia."], [538.6, "And I got this job because I was desperate for money, to be honest."], [544.1, "I had decided, in fact, to live in my van, live in my car for a while."], [550.7, "Now, I did this by choice."], [553.1, "No one forced me to do it, and I could get a job if I wanted to, but I just decided I wanted to live very simply."], [560.4, "So I lived in my van for a while."], [562.8, "And I gave away all my stuff, all my possessions, except for just a few clothes and things."], [570.2, "I wanted to see if I could live very simply, without much money."], [575.6, "However, at some point I had no money at all, and I decided, oh, I better get a job."], [582.5, "I better work a little bit, make some money."], [584.9, "And I had a social work degree; I had a master's in social work."], [588.9, "This was before I got a master's in teaching English."], [592.0, "So it was very easy for me to get a job in social work."], [595.9, "And especially the government agencies always need people."], [599.3, "So I went and I got a job at the Department of Family and Children's Services."], [604.1, "And the pay was, you know, great."], [607.4, "Since I was living so simply, the money was nice; I needed the money."], [613.9, "However, I quickly realized it was a very terrible organization."], [618.6, "And the worst part for me was that the social workers had such terrible attitudes about the people they were supposed to help."], [629.4, "They thought they were stupid, they thought they were, you know, low life."], [635.5, "Low life means a very low status person."], [639.2, "So they just had terrible attitudes about these people"], [644.5, "they were supposed to help."], [646.4, "And they would insult them and belittle them."], [649.0, "Not directly to them."], [650.5, "They wouldn't belittle them directly."], [651.9, "But talking to each other, the social workers would laugh and tell jokes and belittle the poor people who would come in."], [660.5, "And indirectly, they would belittle the poor people themselves, you know, they didn't give them much respect."], [667.2, "They did not give them the same respect that they gave people who had money."], [673.7, "And this bothered me a lot, a lot, a lot."], [677.1, "Because as a social worker and as a teacher,"], [681.4, "I think that respect and equality are very, very important."], [686.2, "And especially as a social worker, because in a social work situation, these people have some serious problems."], [693.1, "They may have economic problems, they don't have much money; they may have physical problems, they may have mental problems."], [700.7, "Anyway, they're in a weak position."], [703.0, "And because they're in a weak position, it's easy to put yourself above them and treat them badly, which is what a lot of these social workers were doing."], [714.1, "But it's wrong to do that, and I felt very strongly it was wrong."], [719.5, "We had to try very hard to keep ourselves equal with them, treat them with the same exact respect we would treat anybody."], [728.3, "You know, I give the same respect to my social work clients as I would to Bill Gates or the president."], [736.3, "In fact, the president... I'd give more respect to the clients."], [740.5, "Anyway, so I started to have kind of arguments with the social workers."], [745.5, "I didn't agree with a lot of their approaches, a lot of their attitudes."], [750.8, "And finally I decided to quit."], [753.1, "I decided I cannot work for this kind of organization that treats people so badly, especially people who need help."], [760.8, "So I quit the job, and eventually I left social work."], [765.0, "And this is mostly the reason."], [767.3, "When I tell people I was a social worker, I worked in the emergency room, or I worked with homeless people,"], [775.3, "and I say, oh, I quit, it was too stressful, everybody always thinks, oh, you quit because those people are terrible."], [783.3, "You quit because it's hard to work with those people."], [786.9, "But that's not true."], [788.5, "That's not why I quit."], [789.8, "I quit because I couldn't work with the other social workers."], [794.0, "It was not the clients that I had problems with."], [797.2, "Yes, the clients could be difficult, but I understood them."], [800.6, "I understood they were having some serious life problems, and I felt respect for them because they were having such trouble and still surviving."], [811.6, "I know that I could not survive some of those situations myself."], [816.1, "I'm not that strong, so I had respect for my clients, even the very difficult ones, but what I could not respect were the other social workers who were so mean."], [827.3, "So superior."], [829.3, "So arrogant."], [831.6, "I just found this again and again and again in many different organizations where they would treat their clients so badly."], [839.7, "They're supposed to be helping them, but instead they're disrespecting them."], [844.2, "And finally, I just decided, you know, I don't like this."], [848.0, "I don't like working in these kinds of organizations."], [851.0, "I want to help people."], [853.4, "I do not want to belittle them."], [855.8, "And at the same time that this was happening, I had gone abroad; I had lived in Japan, I lived in Korea."], [863.4, "And I realized I love traveling, and I loved meeting people from other countries."], [868.2, "And I became more and more interested in teaching."], [871.4, "It was so much fun: every day I would go to school and go to class, and I would meet people from other countries and learn from them, and they would learn from me."], [883.1, "It was just so much fun, I really loved it."], [886.5, "And finally I decided, you know, this is what I really love to do."], [891.4, "Social work's nice, but I love teaching; it's my passion, and this is what I want to do."], [897.6, "And so I went back to school and I got my master's degree in teaching English, and I worked in Thailand for a while and started this website, etc."], [907.6, "Anyway, so there's my life story in a nutshell in a very quick summary."], [912.7, "All right, so that's all for today's commentary for the Lost Custody letter."], [918.1, "More Dear Abby letters will be coming."], [921.5, "And I hope you enjoyed this one."], [923.0, "See you next time."], [923.7, "Bye."]]$j$::jsonb,
  $j$[["custody", "/ˈkʌstədi/", "quyền nuôi con"], ["frightened", "/ˈfraɪtnd/", "hoảng sợ"], ["threat", "/θret/", "mối đe dọa"], ["refuse", "/rɪˈfjuːz/", "từ chối, không chịu"], ["support", "/səˈpɔːt/", "nuôi, chu cấp"], ["belittle", "/bɪˈlɪtl/", "coi thường, hạ thấp"], ["whenever", "/wenˈevə/", "mỗi khi"], ["marriage", "/ˈmærɪdʒ/", "cuộc hôn nhân"], ["honeymoon", "/ˈhʌnimuːn/", "tuần trăng mật"], ["divorce", "/dɪˈvɔːs/", "ly hôn"], ["make sure", "/meɪk ʃɔː/", "đảm bảo"], ["judge", "/dʒʌdʒ/", "thẩm phán"], ["unfit", "/ʌnˈfɪt/", "không đủ tư cách, không phù hợp"], ["my world", "/maɪ wɜːld/", "cả thế giới của tôi"], ["be better off", "/bi ˈbetər ɒf/", "khá hơn, tốt hơn"], ["on disability", "/ɒn ˌdɪsəˈbɪləti/", "đang hưởng trợ cấp khuyết tật"], ["chance", "/tʃɑːns/", "cơ hội, khả năng"], ["stick it out", "/stɪk ɪt aʊt/", "cố chịu đựng đến cùng"], ["take the chance", "/teɪk ðə tʃɑːns/", "liều, chấp nhận rủi ro"], ["trapped", "/træpt/", "mắc kẹt"]]$j$::jsonb,
  $j$[["frightened by threat of lost custody", "hoảng sợ vì bị đe dọa mất quyền nuôi con"], ["have been married 17 months", "đã kết hôn được 17 tháng"], ["a 23-month-old son", "con trai 23 tháng tuổi"], ["refuses to help support our family", "không chịu phụ giúp nuôi gia đình"], ["belittles me whenever he talks to me", "coi thường tôi mỗi khi nói chuyện với tôi"], ["I'm not sure what to do about it", "tôi không biết phải làm gì"], ["he'd make sure he would get custody", "anh ta sẽ đảm bảo giành quyền nuôi con"], ["an unfit mother", "một người mẹ không đủ tư cách"], ["My son is my world.", "Con trai là cả thế giới của tôi."], ["he wouldn't be better off with Derek", "nó sẽ không khá hơn khi ở với Derek"], ["I am on disability", "tôi đang hưởng trợ cấp khuyết tật"], ["a good chance of getting custody", "nhiều cơ hội giành quyền nuôi con"], ["What should I do?", "Tôi nên làm gì?"], ["stick it out with my husband", "cố chịu đựng với chồng"], ["take the chance of losing my son", "liều chấp nhận nguy cơ mất con"]]$j$::jsonb,
  $j$[["S + have been married + (for) + time", "They have been married for twenty years.", "Họ đã kết hôn được hai mươi năm.", "been married"], ["a + number-month/year-old + noun", "She has a three-year-old daughter.", "Cô ấy có một cô con gái ba tuổi.", "year-old|month-old|old"], ["S + hasn't + V3 + for + time", "I haven't seen him for a month.", "Tôi đã không gặp anh ấy một tháng nay.", "hasn't|haven't"], ["S + refuse(s) to + V", "He refuses to say sorry.", "Anh ấy không chịu xin lỗi.", "refuses to|refuse to|refused to"], ["..., whenever + clause", "She smiles whenever she sees him.", "Cô ấy mỉm cười mỗi khi gặp anh ấy.", "whenever"], ["I'm not sure what to + V", "I'm not sure what to say.", "Tôi không biết nên nói gì.", "not sure what"], ["If + S + ever + V-ed, ...", "If you ever need help, call me.", "Nếu bạn có bao giờ cần giúp, hãy gọi tôi.", "ever"], ["S + make(s) sure (that) + clause", "Make sure you lock the door.", "Nhớ khóa cửa nhé.", "make sure|made sure"], ["S + be + my world", "My kids are my world.", "Các con là cả thế giới của tôi.", "my world"], ["S + would be better off + V-ing / with + noun", "You'd be better off taking a taxi.", "Bạn nên đi taxi thì hơn.", "better off"], ["S + have a good chance of + V-ing", "She has a good chance of winning.", "Cô ấy có nhiều khả năng thắng.", "chance of"], ["S + stick it out", "The job is hard, but I'll stick it out.", "Công việc vất vả, nhưng tôi sẽ cố trụ lại.", "stick it out"], ["take the chance of + V-ing", "I don't want to take the chance of missing the train.", "Tôi không muốn liều để lỡ chuyến tàu.", "take the chance|chance of"]]$j$::jsonb
