85.7, "s", "He always nixes this idea."], [488.8, "q", "Does Gabriel agree with the idea?"], [492.4, "a", "No, he nixes the idea, right?"], [494.8, "s", "He shoots the idea down, he destroys the idea, he immediately says no."], [500.0, "s", "He says no, we are not studying English."], [503.1, "s", "He nixes the idea."], [505.8, "q", "Does he nix all of Kathy's ideas?"], [512.0, "a", "No, he doesn't nix all of Kathy's ideas, he only nixes her idea to study English."], [519.3, "s", "When she wants to study English, he always nixes the idea, he always says no immediately."], [524.7, "s", "No, we're not studying English."], [529.1, "q", "Does Gabriel nix Bob's idea?"], [535.2, "a", "No, no, he doesn't nix Bob's idea."], [538.0, "s", "He nixes Kathy's idea, right?"], [541.7, "s", "Gabriel nixes Kathy's idea, not Bob's idea."], [546.0, "q", "And when he nixes her idea, what does he say?"], [550.5, "a", "Well Gabriel always nixes the idea and says, hell no, we're not studying English."], [555.0, "q", "How does Kathy feel when this happens?"], [559.0, "a", "Well, Kathy feels unsettled, right?"], [561.6, "s", "She feels a little strange, a little uncomfortable."], [565.0, "q", "Does she feel very, very, very upset?"], [568.9, "a", "No, she doesn't."], [570.0, "s", "She feels just unsettled, a little upset."], [573.4, "s", "And then when she feels unsettled, she says hesitantly, okay, it's okay."], [582.4, "s", "However, in her head,"], [585.4, "s", "Silently in her head, she is thinking about grammar."], [589.1, "s", "And she is reviewing English grammar."], [591.6, "s", "Then she feels much better."], [594.6, "q", "When she says okay, does she say it strongly and quickly?"], [601.2, "a", "No, no, she does not."], [602.7, "s", "She says it hesitantly."], [605.1, "s", "She stops and she starts."], [606.8, "s", "She's not sure."], [607.5, "s", "Uh, she says, okay."], [612.3, "s", "She says it hesitantly, right?"], [614.7, "s", "When Kathy says okay, she does not say it directly."], [618.0, "s", "She says it very, very hesitantly."], [620.8, "q", "What does she say hesitantly?"], [624.1, "a", "Well, she says, okay, hesitantly."], [629.4, "s", "All right."], [630.5, "s", "So let's go back to the top again."], [632.6, "s", "And this time I'm going to pause after key phrases."], [636.4, "s", "Please copy my pronunciation."], [640.1, "s", "Remember, this is not just repeating words."], [642.4, "s", "Repeating words does not help."], [645.2, "s", "You need to copy my pronunciation."], [647.7, "s", "When I go up, you should go up."], [650.2, "s", "When I go down, you should go down."], [653.8, "s", "Copy my intonation very carefully."], [656.9, "s", "Okay, let's go to the top."], [657.9, "s", "Here we go."], [659.4, "s", "Kathy always obsesses about English."], [670.6, "s", "Good."], [670.6, "s", "He constantly worries about taking the TOEFL test."], [673.6, "s", "When she worries, she always rubs her hands together."], [678.2, "s", "They are always chafed."], [687.0, "s", "Her friend Gabriel is the opposite."], [690.0, "s", "He always opts to remain relaxed."], [701.4, "s", "He always reprimands Kathy."], [705.9, "s", "He always reprimands Kathy."], [710.2, "s", "He says, stop obsessing about English, dammit."], [717.5, "s", "He says, stop obsessing about English, dammit."], [723.5, "s", "Good."], [724.5, "s", "Kathy never stands up to Gabriel."], [729.9, "s", "Kathy never stands up to Gabriel."], [734.6, "s", "She just says, I'm sorry."], [737.0, "s", "Don't be mad."], [738.5, "s", "Then she says, but let's go to the library now and study English, please."], [744.8, "s", "Gabriel always nixes this idea."], [750.7, "s", "Gabriel always nixes this idea."], [755.7, "s", "He says, hell no, we're not studying English."], [760.7, "s", "Kathy feels unsettled."], [765.6, "s", "Kathy feels unsettled."], [769.5, "s", "But she says hesitantly, uh, okay."], [777.1, "s", "But she says hesitantly, uh, okay."], [780.6, "s", "However, in her head, quietly, she starts to review English grammar."], [792.6, "s", "Then she feels much better."], [796.3, "s", "Okay, that is all for this mini-story."], [799.7, "s", "Please pause now and try to tell all of the story yourself."], [805.8, "s", "Say it out loud, not silently."], [807.7, "s", "Say it out loud, speak it out loud."], [809.7, "s", "Try to tell all of the story using the new vocabulary and also using the correct pronunciation, the correct intonation."], [819.1, "s", "If this is difficult, if you cannot do it, no problem."], [822.5, "s", "Just listen again."], [823.9, "s", "Listen again."], [824.9, "s", "Listen again."], [825.9, "s", "Listening is most important."], [827.9, "s", "Okay."], [828.4, "s", "Good luck."], [829.3, "s", "On to the point of view mini stories."], [831.5, "s", "Bye-bye."]]}]$j$::jsonb,
  $j$[[0.5, "Okay, welcome to the Point of View mini-stories for Obsessive Behavior."], [6.6, "Let's get started."], [7.6, "Same story now with three different versions."], [11.3, "First version, 20 years ago."], [14.1, "20 years ago, there was a woman named Kathy."], [18.3, "Kathy always obsessed about English."], [21.0, "She constantly worried about taking the TOEFL test."], [23.8, "When she worried, she always rubbed her hands together."], [28.5, "They were always chafed."], [30.8, "Her friend Gabriel was the opposite."], [33.8, "He always opted to remain relaxed."], [36.6, "He always reprimanded Kathy and said, Kathy, stop obsessing about English, dammit."], [43.8, "Kathy never stood up to Gabriel."], [46.0, "She just said, I'm sorry."], [48.5, "Don't be mad."], [50.1, "Then she said, But, uh, let's go to the library now and study English, please.\""], [56.1, "Gabriel always nixed this idea."], [58.9, "He'd say, \"'Hell no!"], [60.9, "We're not studying English.'\" Kathy felt unsettled, but she said hesitantly, \"'Um, okay.\""], [71.5, "However, in her mind, she would silently start to review English grammar."], [76.3, "Then, she always felt much better."], [81.2, "Alright, let's do the next version."], [83.4, "The next version we'll start with since last year."], [88.9, "Okay, there's a woman named Cathy."], [91.4, "Since last year, she has obsessed about English."], [96.1, "She has constantly worried about taking the TOEFL test."], [100.2, "When she's worried, she has always rubbed her hands together."], [103.8, "They have been chafed every day since last year."], [110.0, "Her friend Gabriel has been the opposite."], [112.6, "He has always opted to remain relaxed."], [116.8, "He has reprimanded Kathy every time she worries, and has said, stop obsessing about English, dammit!"], [125.9, "Kathy has never stood up to Gabriel."], [129.3, "She has always said, I'm sorry, don't be mad at me."], [134.3, "Then she's always said, but, um, let's go to the library now and study English, please."], [141.1, "Gabriel has always nixed this idea."], [144.5, "He's always said, hell no, we're not studying English."], [149.5, "And Kathy has always felt unsettled."], [152.3, "And she's always said, hesitantly, uh, okay."], [157.0, "However, in her head, she has started to review English grammar every time."], [163.7, "Then she has always felt much better."], [169.0, "Okay, now did you notice I used the word always a lot in that version?"], [172.8, "Always, always."], [173.9, "Why?"], [174.5, "Why did I always say always?"], [176.5, "Well, it's because I'm talking about something that started in the past and has continued until now."], [183.0, "So I want to say “always”, because I want to make clear that I'm not talking about one specific time, right?"], [191.5, "I have a conversation here: Kathy, stop obsessing about English, dammit, right?"], [197.3, "He says this, she says that."], [200.3, "Well, if this only happened one time in the past, we would use the simple past, right?"], [206.0, "I would say, Gabriel said, stop obsessing about English; Kathy said, I'm sorry."], [212.0, "But this is a conversation that has happened many times."], [216.7, "They always have the same conversation since last year."], [220.8, "So ten months ago they would have this conversation, and then five months ago they would have the same conversation again, right?"], [231.0, "So always, almost every day, starting one year ago until now, they're always having the same conversation."], [238.9, "So that's why I say “has said”, “has always said”."], [243.7, "“Always” makes it more clear that it's not something that happened one time."], [249.1, "It has happened repeatedly, starting last year, until now."], [256.4, "If it was only one time, it would be the past tense."], [258.8, "But since it has happened many times, starting last year until now, well, then we can say “has said”, “has done”."], [266.7, "Et cetera."], [269.7, "Last version begins with “next year”."], [275.0, "There's a girl named Kathy."], [277.3, "Next year, she will always obsess about English, because next year, she has to take the TOEFL test."], [287.6, "Now, when she'll worry, she'll always rub her hands together."], [292.0, "They're always going to be chafed."], [295.7, "Her friend Gabriel will be the opposite."], [298.2, "He'll always opt to remain relaxed."], [301.2, "He'll always reprimand Kathy and say, Kathy, stop obsessing about English, dammit."], [307.7, "Kathy will never stand up to Gabriel."], [311.1, "She'll just say, I'm sorry, don't be mad."], [316.1, "Then she'll say."], [317.6, "But let's go to the library now and study English."], [321.7, "Gabriel will always nix this idea."], [326.0, "He'll always say, hell no, we're not studying English."], [331.2, "Kathy'll feel unsettled, but she'll say hesitantly, oh, okay."], [338.4, "However, in her mind, she'll silently begin to review English grammar."], [343.6, "Then she'll feel much better."], [347.3, "Okay, those are our three versions."], [351.5, "Please pause now."], [353.4, "Try to tell each version."], [355.5, "So, twenty years ago, since last year, and next year, three versions."], [362.8, "If you can't do it, it's okay."], [364.6, "Listen to one version again."], [366.1, "Listen many times."], [367.6, "Then pause."], [368.7, "Try to tell that version."], [370.6, "And then do it again with the second version, and then again with the third one."], [374.5, "Just listen, listen, listen."], [375.8, "Listening is the most important thing."], [377.9, "Do not become worried and stressed about perfect grammar."], [382.5, "Don't obsess about grammar."], [384.7, "Don't be like Kathy, right?"], [386.6, "Don't obsess about correctness."], [388.7, "Don't obsess about being perfect."], [391.3, "That is not the purpose of this lesson."], [395.9, "Listening is the number one thing you should do."], [399.4, "If you feel relaxed, then okay, pause and try to tell each version yourself."], [406.5, "If you feel stressed, don't do that, just listen."], [410.1, "Okay, that's all."], [411.8, "I'll see you next time."], [413.2, "Bye-bye."]]$j$::jsonb,
  $j$[[0.5, "Hi, welcome to the commentary for Obsessive Behavior."], [5.4, "This is an interesting little Dear Abby letter, quite short."], [10.0, "In fact, you know, I was thinking about the different levels of our members."], [14.2, "Some of you, most of you are advanced learners."], [17.5, "You have to be advanced just to read my homepage in my newsletter and understand it."], [23.4, "So most of you are advanced, probably Dear Abby is pretty easy for you."], [27.5, "But some of you are at a lower level, I would say probably an intermediate level."], [33.6, "And for you, I think the Dear Abby lessons are much better."], [39.4, "The article lessons, the main articles, can be very difficult for some people, especially for intermediate level learners."], [49.2, "And some of you have complained to me, in fact."], [52.3, "That, you know, AJ, these articles are much too difficult for me."], [56.1, "If that's the case, if that's true."], [58.8, "I recommend focusing on the Dear Abby lessons and the pronunciation lessons instead."], [66.3, "They are easier and they're shorter."], [69.1, "So even if it is a little bit difficult, there's still not so much new language to learn like the articles can possibly be."], [78.9, "So for the intermediate level members, I recommend focus on Dear Abby."], [83.9, "It's a great level for you."], [86.0, "And for the advanced learners, I recommend also using Dear Abby, but in a different way."], [90.9, "You'll still learn some new phrases, especially casual English phrases."], [95.3, "But you can also use it to really go deeply with some things that you may already know."], [102.8, "Focus on the mini stories and the point of view mini stories."], [105.8, "And really get this to go deeply by listening again and again and again, many, many times."], [113.3, "You all know the past tense, for example."], [116.4, "However, when you speak, many of you may make mistakes with the past tense."], [121.4, "You may use the present tense when you should use the past."], [125.9, "I know many of my San Francisco students do this."], [128.9, "Now, if I give them a test, they will all do very well on the past tense."], [133.8, "Yet, when we actually speak, they don't use it."], [137.8, "What's the problem here?"], [139.6, "Well, the problem is, knowing and doing are totally different."], [144.5, "Knowing is a left-brain activity, it's an analytical activity."], [148.9, "It means you analyze, you break it apart, you think about it."], [154.8, "That's one kind of knowledge."], [157.3, "But using a language, being able to speak English fluently, is a different kind of knowledge."], [165.1, "It's more like playing a sport."], [168.1, "Okay, you can know about basketball."], [171.0, "You can study basketball."], [172.9, "It does not mean you can actually play basketball, right?"], [176.8, "Studying basketball and playing basketball are totally different."], [180.7, "What's the same thing with English?"], [183.2, "Studying about English, learning the rules, conjugating verbs, taking tests, that is totally different than using English, speaking English fluently."], [193.6, "Understanding quickly."], [195.4, "Speaking quickly and correctly."], [198.0, "Totally different than studying the grammar and taking the tests."], [201.7, "This is why I think that obsessive test-taking and obsessing about English in general is very, very bad."], [210.8, "I have a lot of students in San Francisco, for example, who obsess about the TOEFL test."], [216.6, "That's why my little mini story, it's kind of a joke about those students."], [221.2, "Not a mean joke, it's a nice joke, but it's true, there are a lot of students like this, in Japan, oh my God."], [228.1, "So many students like this, they're focused on the TOEIC, or the TOEFL."], [233.7, "Or the university exam."], [237.5, "Or a grad school exam, but it's always a test."], [241.3, "A lot of the people don't seem to care about actually learning English and using it."], [247.7, "Or about countries that speak English, America, or Australia, or England."], [252.4, "They're not interested in the cultures, or the people, or communication."], [256.2, "It's just this damn test."], [259.1, "And they think that if they focus on the test,"], [262.0, "And they do well on the test, that means, ah, now I can use English, now I can speak English."], [268.2, "And they're always very shocked that even if they get a good score on the test,"], [274.9, "When they actually try to understand Americans, for example, they can't."], [280.3, "They can't."], [280.9, "So many students come here to San Francisco."], [283.8, "They have a good TOEFL exam."], [286.0, "They go on to the street, and someone chats with them, talks with them at a bus stop, and they're confused and they don't know what they're saying."], [295.0, "And when they try to talk, they speak very slowly."], [298.2, "They can't remember."], [299.9, "They're trying to conjugate verbs and translate."], [303.6, "Their pronunciation is terrible and Americans don't understand them."], [308.2, "They try to say something and Americans are confused."], [311.4, "What?"], [311.9, "Huh?"], [312.4, "What?"], [312.8, "Huh?"], [313.7, "And that's a terrible feeling."], [315.4, "I know."], [316.1, "I had that feeling in Thailand."], [318.6, "I would try to use Thai."], [320.6, "People could not understand me."], [322.4, "I became very upset and frustrated."], [325.8, "So you have to separate these ideas."], [329.3, "They're different."], [331.1, "This idea of studying English, learning the rules, memorizing the grammar, and taking tests."], [339.0, "That's one thing."], [341.0, "And then the other thing is speaking English fluently, using it correctly."], [346.0, "Knowing the grammar deep down."], [348.5, "Feeling what is correct."], [351.0, "Feeling, not knowing, feeling when something sounds correct and when it sounds wrong."], [359.0, "Speaking with good pronunciation."], [361.8, "Speaking without translating."], [363.1, "Now, that is a different kind of knowledge, it's a skill, and they don't mix very much."], [370.7, "The test-taking, analyzing, memorizing part, it's good if you want to, you know, get a PhD and talk about English in your own language."], [381.5, "But it's not very good if you actually want to use English."], [386.8, "So you've got to focus on using English."], [390.4, "And if you want to use English well, just do not obsess about tests."], [395.4, "The students who obsess about the TOEFL exam, for example, they're always my worst students."], [401.0, "I mean, 95% of the time, when I have a student who is obsessed about... “obsessed” is kind of the emotion, or I can use it as a verb, “to obsess”."], [413.3, "So if I have a student who obsesses, using it as a verb."], [416.6, "who obsesses about the TOEFL exam, for example,"], [420.6, "I know they're probably going to be quite low."], [425.3, "And I also know they're going to learn very slowly."], [429.9, "Because if they obsess about the test, that's all they will focus on."], [436.0, "And when they obsess about the test, it makes them use different kinds of study methods."], [441.1, "What do they do?"], [442.3, "Well, they study grammar, grammar, so much grammar, grammar books, grammar books, grammar books."], [447.1, "What else do they do?"], [448.3, "They go buy those TOEFL test books, and they take practice TOEFL tests, again, and again, and again."], [456.3, "And that's all they do."], [457.9, "And that's not natural English, and it's a terrible way to learn a language, in general, any language."], [466.2, "So in fact, their TOEFL scores do not improve a lot."], [470.5, "Their general speaking ability does not improve at all, usually."], [478.5, "And then what else happens?"], [479.4, "I have another person in my class who's just really interested in American culture."], [484.3, "They really like being here, and they really want to meet people, and they're excited: wow, English is interesting, and oh, I love English movies, and oh, American books are really kind of cool."], [498.7, "And they like certain movies and music and culture, and blah."], [503.4, "Those people learn very quickly."], [505.5, "They learn fast."], [506.8, "Why?"], [507.2, "Well, number one, they're listening a lot."], [510.2, "They're listening to American movies all the time, they're making American friends, so they're listening to Americans speak English constantly."], [518.7, "These people usually study on their own in a different way."], [523.4, "They listen to podcasts."], [525.1, "They listen to these kinds of lessons on Effortless English."], [529.3, "They listen to TV shows."], [532.6, "That's how they study."], [534.2, "The funny thing is that these people also improve on their TOEFL exams."], [540.1, "And they improve faster than the people who are obsessed with the TOEFL."], [545.8, "And that makes the TOEFL people so frustrated and angry sometimes, right?"], [551.1, "They're, here they are, they're studying the TOEFL exam."], [553.8, "They're focused on the TOEFL."], [555.1, "They're obsessed with the TOEFL, and somebody else is having fun,"], [560.7, "and the person who has fun improves faster and they get a better TOEFL score."], [566.5, "And oh, they get so upset, some of these students."], [570.4, "They come to me AJ."], [572.6, "Oh my TOEFL."], [574.3, "I took it again and it didn't improve very much."], [578.1, "Why am I not getting better?"], [581.0, "And Seiko over here, she's having fun and making friends and just listening to movies and podcasts."], [589.3, "And she did better than me."], [591.5, "I don't understand."], [594.0, "And then I tell them, well, you know, I try to explain the effortless English method."], [599.7, "I try to explain research."], [602.0, "But unfortunately, these students, they obsess so much"], [608.6, "with the exam."], [610.6, "They can't hear me."], [612.3, "It's frustrating for me."], [614.1, "I try to tell them, you know, please study in a different way."], [617.5, "Learn in a different way."], [619.0, "You will have more fun."], [621.1, "Your speaking will improve."], [622.8, "And you will even do better on the TOEFL exam than you are now."], [628.0, "But they don't listen."], [629.3, "I mean, the students who are really obsessed about exams, they will not listen."], [633.8, "It's like a mental disease."], [636.4, "I don't know."], [637.1, "It's kind of like some kind of craziness."], [640.8, "They're so obsessed, it's all they can think about."], [643.7, "So I tell them, try the other method."], [645.8, "Try Seiko's method."], [646.6, "It's working for her."], [648.1, "But they won't listen."], [649.3, "They'll say, yeah, okay, okay."], [651.2, "And the next day I see them."], [652.8, "They have their TOEFL exam book."], [654.9, "And they're studying the grammar again."], [657.6, "And then two months later, they take the TOEFL exam."], [660.4, "Oh no, I didn't improve very much."], [663.7, "Oh, they come back to me: AJ, what should I do?"], [668.8, "It's the same conversation every time."], [671.6, "I have this one student who's Korean, who has been doing this for over one year, over one year obsessed with the TOEFL exam."], [682.8, "Takes the test, does not improve enough, comes back to me, complains: AJ, my TOEFL not good enough, what should I do?"], [691.7, "Every time I tell her, well, you should listen to podcasts, you should read interesting books that you like, for fun, you know, all the Effortless English methods."], [703.1, "I tell her, please do this, it will help you, trust me."], [708.0, "And every time she says uh-huh, and then she ignores me and she uses the same method again."], [715.3, "I see her using her TOEFL book, studying grammar."], [719.0, "And then two months later she takes the TOEFL test again, and then: oh God, AJ, my TOEFL test, it didn't go up enough, I didn't improve very much, what should I do, what's wrong?"], [733.2, "I mean, again and again, this has been happening for one year, and she still will not change her methods."], [741.3, "And this is really a form of insanity, of craziness."], [745.4, "When you keep doing the same thing,"], [747.7, "and you keep getting a bad result,"], [750.6, "and you keep using the same method, and you think maybe this time my result will be different."], [757.8, "But again, you get the same result."], [760.5, "It's a kind of craziness."], [763.2, "And I don't know why, but these exams, these tests can sometimes make people crazy like this."], [769.0, "So my advice to you: if you need the TOEFL exam, you know, it's okay."], [773.6, "Some of you need it."], [774.7, "Some of you want to study grad school in America, for example."], [779.4, "That's fine."], [780.4, "Nothing wrong with that."], [781.8, "But don't obsess about the test."], [783.5, "It will hurt you."], [784.8, "You will learn more slowly if you do that."], [788.0, "Use the Effortless English methods."], [790.4, "I guarantee you will learn faster than people who are taking TOEFL classes and studying TOEFL books."], [797.8, "Your TOEFL scores will improve better and more and faster than the people who are studying the TOEFL test."], [807.6, "I promise you, this is not just my opinion."], [810.3, "There's a lot of research about this."], [813.0, "The people who focus on the test actually do worse on the test."], [817.6, "The people who use effortless methods also do better on tests like the TOEFL exam or the TOEIC."], [825.7, "So, you know, you have more fun."], [828.8, "You learn faster."], [830.1, "You do better on the tests."], [831.8, "Your pronunciation is better, you become more fluent."], [835.1, "Everything is better if you use an effortless system."], [839.0, "So don't obsess about these tests, please."], [842.0, "Put your TOEFL books to the side, put away your grammar books, use an effortless method."], [847.3, "And you will do much better."], [849.5, "All right, here's another pep talk for today, I guess."], [852.2, "That's the end of the commentary for Obsessive Behavior."], [855.6, "I'll see you on the next lesson."], [857.0, "Bye."]]$j$::jsonb,
  $j$[["obsessive", "/əbˈsesɪv/", "ám ảnh"], ["behavior", "/bɪˈheɪvjə/", "hành vi"], ["deny", "/dɪˈnaɪ/", "phủ nhận"], ["probable", "/ˈprɒbəbl/", "có khả năng, có thể"], ["cause", "/kɔːz/", "nguyên nhân"], ["nephew", "/ˈnefjuː/", "cháu trai"], ["have problems with", "/hæv ˈprɒbləmz wɪð/", "gặp vấn đề với"], ["professional", "/prəˈfeʃənl/", "chuyên môn, chuyên nghiệp"], ["suspect", "/səˈspekt/", "nghi ngờ"], ["suffer from", "/ˈsʌfə frɒm/", "mắc (bệnh), chịu đựng"], ["obsessive-compulsive disorder", "/əbˌsesɪv kəmˈpʌlsɪv dɪsˈɔːdə/", "rối loạn ám ảnh cưỡng chế"], ["refuse", "/rɪˈfjuːz/", "không chịu"], ["get help", "/ɡet help/", "được giúp đỡ, đi chữa trị"], ["chafed", "/tʃeɪft/", "trầy xước (do cọ xát)"], ["raw", "/rɔː/", "rát đỏ, trầy da"], ["reprimand", "/ˈreprɪmɑːnd/", "quở trách"], ["nix", "/nɪks/", "gạt bỏ, bác bỏ"], ["issue", "/ˈɪʃuː/", "vấn đề"], ["suggest", "/səˈdʒest/", "cho thấy, gợi ý"], ["stand up to", "/stænd ʌp tuː/", "dám chống lại, phản đối"], ["worried", "/ˈwʌrid/", "lo lắng"], ["aunt", "/ɑːnt/", "cô, dì"]]$j$::jsonb,
  $j$[["deny probable cause", "phủ nhận nguyên nhân có thể có"], ["has had problems with obsessive hand washing for years", "đã gặp vấn đề rửa tay ám ảnh nhiều năm"], ["I have enough professional experience", "tôi có đủ kinh nghiệm chuyên môn"], ["strongly suspect that", "nghi ngờ mạnh mẽ rằng"], ["he suffers from", "cháu mắc chứng"], ["refuse to believe their child has a problem", "không chịu tin con mình có vấn đề"], ["will not take him to get help", "không đưa cháu đi chữa trị"], ["chafed and raw", "trầy xước và rát đỏ"], ["reprimand him for this behavior", "quở trách cháu vì hành vi này"], ["he really needs professional help", "cháu thật sự cần giúp đỡ chuyên môn"], ["the biggest problem", "vấn đề lớn nhất"], ["nixes all issues that suggest a problem", "gạt phăng mọi dấu hiệu cho thấy có vấn đề"], ["will not stand up to her", "không dám phản đối cô ấy"], ["What can I do?", "Tôi có thể làm gì?"]]$j$::jsonb,
  $j$[["S + have/has had problems with + noun/V-ing + for years", "She has had problems with sleeping for years.", "Cô ấy bị khó ngủ nhiều năm nay.", "had problems with"], ["S + have enough + noun + to + V", "I have enough money to buy a car.", "Tôi có đủ tiền để mua xe.", "enough"], ["S + strongly suspect(s) that + clause", "I strongly suspect that he is lying.", "Tôi rất nghi là anh ta đang nói dối.", "suspect"], ["S + suffer(s) from + noun", "My grandmother suffers from back pain.", "Bà tôi bị đau lưng.", "suffers from|suffer from"], ["S + refuse to believe (that) + clause", "She refuses to believe that he's gone.", "Cô ấy không chịu tin rằng anh ấy đã ra đi.", "refuse to believe|refuses to believe"], ["S + take + someone + to get help", "We took him to get help at the hospital.", "Chúng tôi đưa anh ấy đến bệnh viện chữa trị.", "to get help"], ["S + reprimand + someone + for + noun/V-ing", "The boss reprimanded him for being late.", "Sếp quở trách anh ấy vì đi muộn.", "reprimand|reprimanded"], ["..., when + S + really + V", "He stays home when he really needs to work.", "Anh ấy ở nhà trong khi thật ra cần phải đi làm.", "when"], ["S + be + the biggest problem", "Money is the biggest problem.", "Tiền là vấn đề lớn nhất.", "biggest problem"], ["S + will not stand up to + someone", "He never stands up to his boss.", "Anh ấy không bao giờ dám cãi sếp.", "stand up to"], ["What can I do?", "My son won't study. What can I do?", "Con trai tôi không chịu học. Tôi có thể làm gì?", "what can i do"]]$j$::jsonb
